WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.180
To learn what NGF does in a living animal, you remove it.

2
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:08.920
But then the neurons die.

3
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:10.860
What now?

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.120
Without NGF, most of these neurons die.

5
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.620
Their loss muddies any comparison.

6
00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:20.713
Deppmann, Ginty and colleagues found a way round it.

7
00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:22.733
They used mice lacking Bax.

8
00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:25.273
Bax is the pro-death gene.

9
00:00:26.293 --> 00:00:29.593
Without it, sympathetic neurons survive with or without NGF.

10
00:00:30.683 --> 00:00:33.783
So you can compare two ganglia that both keep their neurons.

11
00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:36.853
One comes from a mouse lacking Bax.

12
00:00:37.413 --> 00:00:40.953
The other comes from a mouse lacking both Bax and NGF.

13
00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:46.293
Because both keep their neurons, any difference between them points to NGF.

14
00:00:47.417 --> 00:00:50.917
RNA from whole ganglia was compared on microarrays.

15
00:00:51.857 --> 00:00:54.257
Genes lower without NGF became candidates.

16
00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:00.260
Candidates were then checked by staining, by in situ hybridization, and in culture

17
00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:01.740
with and without NGF.

18
00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:08.233
The list held transcription factors, synaptic genes, genes for metabolism and cell

19
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:10.493
size, and genes for axon growth.

20
00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:13.313
Now think back to the two neurons.

21
00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:16.473
Which kind of gene could make a rich-get-richer loop?

22
00:01:22.883 --> 00:01:25.803
One whose product helps a neuron catch more NGF.

23
00:01:25.803 --> 00:01:29.163
And one gene on the list was the receptor itself.

24
00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:32.827
NGF raises and maintains TrkA.

25
00:01:33.647 --> 00:01:36.627
In newborn ganglia, TrkA fell without NGF.

26
00:01:37.617 --> 00:01:41.937
In cultured neurons, TrkA rose with NGF and fell without it.

27
00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:47.890
After long deprivation, signaling downstream of TrkA was greatly reduced too.

28
00:01:48.883 --> 00:01:54.283
Neurons deprived only briefly could still sustain signaling when NGF returned.

29
00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:59.913
So a neuron that catches NGF makes more of the receptor that catches it.

30
00:02:00.573 --> 00:02:01.733
That is the first loop.

31
00:02:02.683 --> 00:02:04.243
But remember the two neurons.

32
00:02:05.123 --> 00:02:10.083
With capture and synthesis both in proportion to receptor, this loop alone keeps the

33
00:02:10.083 --> 00:02:11.083
shares where they started.

34
00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:14.547
Two other genes on the list matter later.

35
00:02:15.487 --> 00:02:20.027
BDNF and NT-4, neurotrophins that can kill through p75.

36
00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:25.187
The same two genotypes also answer a question about innervation.

37
00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:27.747
The last chapter returns to it.

38
00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:33.130
Takeaway. The Bax trick keeps neurons alive without NGF.

39
00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.430
It showed that NGF raises its own receptor.
